Report where the shared-cache mapping stops on the way into the kernel (enchilada instrument).

launchd is refused twice by shared_region_check_np with ENOMEM, which means the task owns a
shared region whose sr_first_mapping is still -1: no mapping was ever established. On k136 dyld
finally asked for the mapping and the log carries map_and_slide_2 files=54 mappings=62 -- and
then nothing. The line that would follow it, setup error=, never appears, so the machine stopped
either in proc_fdlock() or inside shared_region_map_and_slide_setup().

Bracketing every step is the only way to tell those apart from a log that dies mid-call. map enter
fires before the fd lock; map rdir reports the root directory the shared region must match once
the lock is released; setup sr reports the shared region, its root dir and sr_first_mapping;
setup file reports each file walked, before its descriptor is resolved; map file brackets the call
that installs the mappings; first mapping reports the address that becomes sr_first_mapping; exit
reports what the syscall handed back to dyld.

The exit line exists because the enter/setup/mapping lines alone cannot separate two states that
look identical from the outside: dyld maps the cache and then dies before main, and dyld is
refused after the setup succeeded. Only the return value tells them apart, and it is the one
number the earlier probes never printed. They are diagnostics only: no return value, no state and
no policy changes, and each line is prefixed NATIVE_SR so it can be filtered out of a boot log.

